## The Setting Multiplier at 1ct — Where the Overpay Should Have Gone

The $1,800 overpay on the stone could buy a full setting upgrade. Here is the math side by side.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Stone</th><th>Setting</th><th>Total Ring</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Overpaying buyer</strong></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29992877?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$7,681 D-VVS1 Ideal</a></td><td>$1,000 basic prong</td><td>$8,681</td><td>$7,681 stone, $1,000 setting. Reads as "$8,000 ring" from a distance.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Smart buyer</strong></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30259333?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$4,393 H-VS2 Ideal</a></td><td>$4,000 platinum pavé halo</td><td>$8,393</td><td>$4,393 stone, $4,000 setting. Reads as "$12,000 ring" to the eye.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lab-arbitrage buyer</strong></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29389739?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$629 lab E-VVS2</a></td><td>$3,000 three-stone platinum</td><td>$3,629</td><td>Reads as "$5,000+ ring." Half the budget of overpaying buyer.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** The $1,800 overpay on the stone translates directly into a lost setting upgrade. At 1ct, the setting carries half or more of the ring's perceived value. Spend the saved money on platinum, pavé, or a halo. The ring that comes out beats any max-spec stone in a plain prong on the hand.

